WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.265
Teacher midterm practice, question 6.

2
00:00:03.885 --> 00:00:07.693
A smooth block of copper is pressed against a smooth block of nickel,

3
00:00:07.693 --> 00:00:12.491
heated below their melting points, and held for a long time. What happens?

4
00:00:14.191 --> 00:00:16.929
At the interface, copper goes from one hundred

5
00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:19.558
percent on one side to zero on the other.

6
00:00:19.558 --> 00:00:22.679
So copper has a gradient pointing into the nickel.

7
00:00:23.299 --> 00:00:26.936
Nickel has the mirror image gradient, pointing into the copper.

8
00:00:27.556 --> 00:00:30.546
At high temperature both kinds of atoms are mobile,

9
00:00:30.546 --> 00:00:33.536
so both diffuse, each down its own gradient.

10
00:00:33.536 --> 00:00:37.776
That is interdiffusion: the classic copper nickel diffusion couple.

11
00:00:39.376 --> 00:00:43.860
The answer is C: copper diffuses into the nickel, and nickel into the copper.

12
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.962
Diffusion is not one way, and the melting point does not decide the direction.

13
00:00:49.962 --> 00:00:54.116
Complete solid solubility is exactly why they can interdiffuse.
